<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stepan Shaumian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Stepan Shahumyan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stepan Shaumian was an Armenian Bolshevik who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> Commune, the Soviet government of Baku, in 1918. When the commune fell in July 1918 he and other leaders were arrested; they were shot by anti-Bolshevik forces in the Transcaspian desert in September 1918, remembered in Soviet history as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/26-baku-commissars">26 Baku Commissars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stepan-shaumian.jpg" alt="Stepan Shaumian" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepan_Shahumyan_youth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stepan Shaumian was born on 1 October 1878 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> to a family of Armenian cloth merchants, descended from the Shaumian line of meliks of Meghri. He became involved in revolutionary politics as a student, entered the Riga Polytechnic Institute and in 1899 formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s first Marxist group in the village of Jalaloghli, today Stepanavan. Expelled from the institute in March 1902, arrested and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, he co-founded the Union of Armenian Social Democrats in Tiflis. He studied philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, met Lenin, Julius Martov and Georgi Plekhanov, and sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>. By 1907 he headed the Bolshevik movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> was also active and the two clashed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shaumian led a general strike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> in 1914 and was imprisoned until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> he became Commissar Extraordinary for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and chairman of the Baku Council of People's Commissars, heading a government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, Left Socialist-Revolutionaries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and Dashnaks. At the end of March 1918 the disarming of Azerbaijani soldiers led to fighting in which the commune, supported by Dashnak forces, attacked the Muslim quarters. Estimates of the Azerbaijanis and other Muslims killed range from 3,000 to 12,000. In September 1918 an Ottoman-led Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> retook Baku and killed an estimated 10,000 to 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>.</p><p>Shaumian was ordered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to refuse British help, though he understood what refusing it might cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. On 26 July 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> were outvoted 259 to 236 in the Baku Soviet, and he left the city, saying he and his comrades were forced to leave. A new government, the Central Caspian Dictatorship, was formed as British forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lionel-dunsterville">Lionel Dunsterville</a> arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 31 July the commissars sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, but their ships were captured on 16 August and they were jailed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. When Baku fell in mid-September they escaped or were released and sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy">Krasnovodsk</a>, where anti-Bolshevik forces arrested them; they were executed on the night of 20 September 1918 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> desert. The British role is disputed: Captain Reginald Teague-Jones later claimed he left before the decision was made. The Soviet government made Shaumian a hero, and Khankendi was renamed Stepanakert and Jalaloghli Stepanavan. In January 2009 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> demolished the 26 Commissars Memorial in Baku, which caused an outcry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stepan Shaumian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April it formed the Baku Council of People's Commissars under <b>Stepan Shaumian</b>, part of the Russian SFSR.</blockquote><a href="/state/baku-commune">Baku Commune</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in Krasnovodsky Uyezd. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stepan Shaumian"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5934 3944h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5934 3944h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
